The folder shape is unchanged. The two source files (`index.ts`,
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`) and their two co-located test files all
get rewritten in place — no new helpers, no new subfolders. The `todo.aide`
stays untouched (it tracked the spec-rewrite cycle, which is complete).

## Prerequisites

**`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** must export the new minimal schema before
any step in this plan can typecheck. Specifically, the contract this plan
consumes:

- `BrainAideConfig` (in `@/types/index.js`) shape collapses to:
  `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command: string; args: string[] } }`.
  No `connector`, no `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools`.
- `parseBrainAide(root: string)` reads from `join(root, ".aide", "config",
  "brain.aide")` (the parser's path moves with the file).
- `parseBrainAideFromString(content: string)` validates required fields
  `name`, `mcpServerConfig.command`, `mcpServerConfig.args` — and rejects any
  presence of the deprecated fields (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
  `tools`) as `malformed-frontmatter`.
- `interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]` walks
  `config.mcpServerConfig.args` substituting any `${<key>}` against
  top-level frontmatter fields (today only `${name}` resolves; the default
  scaffold uses no placeholders so the helper is a no-op for default
  installs).

If `parseBrainAide`'s type changes have not yet landed when execution
starts, every step in this plan will fail to typecheck — Step 1 (the
template rewrite) cannot be verified by its tests, Step 2 (the orchestrator
edit) imports the parser. The orchestrator must execute
`src/service/parseBrainAide/`'s plan first.

## Plan

### 1. Rewrite the canonical Obsidian template

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md`

- [ ] 1a. Rewrite `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`.
      Function signature is unchanged: `default function obsidianBrainAideTemplate(rootPath: string): string`.
      The returned string must contain:

      - Frontmatter fence opener `---\n`.
      - YAML body with EXACTLY two top-level fields, in this order:
        - `name: obsidian` (literal label only — the package never branches
          on it; see brain-aide.md "name is descriptive metadata, never
          dispatched on")
        - `mcpServerConfig:` with nested `command` + `args`, platform-conditional:
          - **Windows (`platform() === "win32"`):**
            `command: cmd`
            `args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "<rootPath>"]`
          - **POSIX (any other platform):**
            `command: npx`
            `args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "<rootPath>"]`
      - **No `${rootPath}` placeholder.** The vault path is the literal
        `rootPath` parameter value, written byte-for-byte into the last
        `args` element. The default scaffold does NOT exercise the
        `${...}` substitution surface.
      - **No `connector` field. No `rootPath` field. No `entryFile` field.
        No `tools` block.** Anything beyond `name` and `mcpServerConfig`
        will be rejected by the parser as `malformed-frontmatter`.
      - Frontmatter fence closer `---\n\n`.
      - `## Prose\n\n` heading.
      - The existing prose body (already correct in the current file) —
        carry it forward unchanged. Sentinel phrases must remain:
        `mcp__brain__read_note`, `mcp__brain__search_notes`, `CLAUDE.md`,
        `wikilink`. The prose is independent of the frontmatter schema and
        does not need to change.

      Update the function's JSDoc to reflect the new schema:
      - This is the SINGLE source of canonical Obsidian brain.aide bytes.
      - Frontmatter is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig` — minimum schema is
        the maximum schema.
      - Path is inlined into args byte-for-byte; the package does not own
        the `${rootPath}` substitution path.
      - Platform branching is isolated here and nowhere else in this module.
      - Remove the JSDoc paragraph about `${rootPath}` being a literal
        placeholder — the new template has no placeholders at all.

- [ ] 1b. Rewrite `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts`.
      Keep the existing test scaffolding (vi.mock for `node:os`, dynamic
      import via `getTemplate(rootPath, mockedPlatform?)`,
      `writeBrainAide(root, content)` helper, temp-dir lifecycle) but
      rewrite the assertions for the minimal schema.

      Test groups (each in its own `describe` block):

      - **Round-trip parses cleanly under the new schema.** Write the
        template output to a temp `.aide/config/brain.aide`, call
        `parseBrainAide(<root>)`, assert `result.kind === "ok"`, assert
        `result.config.name === "obsidian"`. Run the same content through
        `parseBrainAideFromString` and assert the two results are
        deep-equal. The round-trip is the load-bearing invariant.

      - **Parsed config contains EXACTLY the new fields.** Assert
        `result.config.name === "obsidian"`. Assert
        `result.config.mcpServerConfig.command` is a non-empty string and
        `result.config.mcpServerConfig.args` is a non-empty string array.
        Assert (via TypeScript or runtime hasOwnProperty) that
        `result.config` has no `connector`, no `rootPath`, no `entryFile`,
        no `tools` keys.

      - **Path is inlined, no placeholder.** Given `rootPath: "/foo/my-vault"`,
        assert the LAST element of `result.config.mcpServerConfig.args`
        equals `"/foo/my-vault"` byte-for-byte (NOT `"${rootPath}"`,
        NOT `"${name}"`). Assert the args array contains no element
        matching `/\$\{.+\}/` — the default scaffold has zero placeholders.

      - **Platform branching — Windows.** Mock `node:os` `platform()` to
        return `"win32"`. Assert `result.config.mcpServerConfig.command ===
        "cmd"`. Assert `result.config.mcpServerConfig.args` equals
        `["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", rootPath]`.

      - **Platform branching — POSIX.** Mock `platform()` to return
        `"linux"`. Assert
        `result.config.mcpServerConfig.command === "npx"`. Assert
        `result.config.mcpServerConfig.args` equals
        `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", rootPath]`.

      - **Prose body sentinel phrases preserved.** Parse the template,
        assert `result.prose` is non-empty and contains
        `mcp__brain__read_note`, `mcp__brain__search_notes`, `CLAUDE.md`,
        and `wikilink`. (This guards the prose against accidental
        regressions during the frontmatter rewrite.)

      - **interpolateArgs is a no-op on the default scaffold.** Parse the
        template, call `interpolateArgs(result.config)`, assert the
        returned array is deep-equal to
        `result.config.mcpServerConfig.args`. The default scaffold has no
        `${...}` placeholders so substitution must be a passthrough — this
        is the regression anchor that the substitution surface stays
        opt-in.

      - **Schema does not include intent-spec or deprecated fields.** Slice
        the frontmatter block out of the raw template string. Assert the
        block does NOT match `/^scope:/m`, `/^outcomes:/m`, `/^intent:/m`,
        `/^status:/m`, `/^connector:/m`, `/^rootPath:/m`, `/^entryFile:/m`,
        `/^tools:/m`. Assert the block DOES match `/^name:/m` and
        `/^mcpServerConfig:/m`.

      Drop the `parseBrainAideFromString produces the same ok result as
      writing to disk` test if it duplicates the round-trip group above —
      otherwise keep it.

