## Decisions

- **The path move is the single load-bearing change in `index.ts`.** Every
  other behavior in the orchestrator — five-step return order, presence
  checks for the seed files, derivation of the MCP entry from the parsed
  brain.aide, the four migration branches in `buildBrainMcpStep` — is
  already correct and stays untouched. Doing more would be scope creep;
  doing less leaves the file pointing at the legacy path. The directory
  boundary is the entire architectural shift, encoded as a one-line path
  rename plus a Tier 3 narration of the directory-wide rule. This matches
  the playbook's foundations/conventions guidance to make changes locally
  rather than reaching across modules.

- **The directory-wide invariant is codified in JSDoc and an inline
  comment, not in a runtime guard.** The spec is explicit that the rule
  is structural ("any file scaffolded into `.aide/config/` is owned by
  the user the moment it lands"). The current `buildBrainAideStep`
  already enforces it for brain.aide by never returning
  `would-overwrite`. Adding a runtime "if the path falls under
  `.aide/config/`, force the status to be one of `exists` or
  `would-create`" guard would shift the rule from a structural property
  of every step that writes there into a runtime check that any future
  helper could also bypass. The Tier 3 inline comment + the helper's
  JSDoc + the spec's `outcomes.undesired` clause are the three places a
  future reviewer can intercept a regression. A runtime guard would
  silently mask the bug instead of stopping it at code review.

- **The template helper rewrite is a minimal-content rewrite, not a
  signature change.** `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(rootPath: string):
  string` keeps its shape — the orchestrator's call site is unchanged,
  the platform-branching mock pattern in tests is unchanged, the
  round-trip-through-parseBrainAide invariant is unchanged. Only the
  emitted bytes change: minimum two-field frontmatter, inline path,
  `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher. This minimizes the orchestrator's
  exposure to the template's internals.

- **Test pipeline anchors to the parser+interpolator round-trip, not to
  hardcoded launcher strings.** The 5c (Windows + POSIX) test computes
  `expectedArgs` by parsing the template and calling `interpolateArgs`.
  This means a future template change (e.g. the launcher gets renamed
  again, or an extra arg is added) propagates through the round-trip
  without churning the test fixture. The platform-branching template
  tests in step 1b assert the literal args (anchoring at the source),
  which catches a template bug; the orchestrator tests in step 3 assert
  what derives from the template (anchoring at the integration), which
  catches a derivation bug. Two layers of anchor, no duplication.

- **No new helper subfolder.** `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` already
  exists with one file (~50 lines). The rewrite shrinks the function
  body slightly (no `${rootPath}` placeholder construction; minimal
  frontmatter) so the file size moves the right direction. The
  progressive-disclosure threshold for splitting (~50 lines per helper
  with dedicated test coverage) is unchanged; nothing in this rewrite
  pushes us toward a sub-helper decomposition.

- **The `parseBrainAide` type/path move is the single hard prerequisite.**
  It is its own plan in its own module — not folded into this plan and
  not duplicated here. The orchestrator (`/aide:build`) executes
  prerequisite plans first; if it does not, this plan's tests fail in
  diagnosable ways (every parser round-trip rejects the new schema) so
  the hard-stop is structural, not silent.

- **No code is added to handle the legacy `.aide/brain.aide` path.** The
  spec's `outcomes.undesired` does not call out a back-compat surface,
  the brain-aide.md methodology doc names exactly one canonical path
  (`.aide/config/brain.aide`), and adding a fallback ("if the new path
  is missing, also check the legacy path") would carry the legacy
  ownership boundary forward indefinitely. A user with a legacy
  brain.aide at `.aide/brain.aide` will see the seed step report
  `would-create` for the new path, scaffold the new file, and have the
  legacy file as an orphan they can delete on their own — same shape as
  any other one-time path move in the package.
